Other intervals such as $\frac{\pi}{2}\le x\le\frac{3\pi}{2}$ also work for sine; mathematicians simply agreed on the simplest choice.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-7.4, xmax=7.4, ymin=-2.6, ymax=1.7, samples=150, clip=false, width=12cm, height=7cm, xtick={-6.2832,-3.1416,-1.5708,1.5708,3.1416,6.2832}, xticklabels={$-2\pi$,$-\pi$,$-\frac{\pi}{2}$,$\frac{\pi}{2}$,$\pi$,$2\pi$}, ytick={-1,1}, every tick label/.style={font=\scriptsize}]
\addplot[thick, <->, domain=-7.4:7.4] {sin(deg(x))};
\addplot[very thick, red, domain=-1.5708:1.5708] {sin(deg(x))};
\addplot[dashed, domain=-7.4:7.4] {0.6} node[pos=0.03, above] {$y=c$};
\node[red, font=\small] at (axis cs:0,-2.2) {one-to-one piece: $-\frac{\pi}{2}\le x\le\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

